{"black_box_warning":{"current_value":"Boxed warning text is populated but uses a DailyMed source, contains duplicated heading text, and includes internally inconsistent/typographic wording such as CARIOVASCULAR, per minutes, and pediatric 1 to 3 mg/min in part of the text.","fda_source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb%22&limit=5","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"],"proposed_value":"WARNING: CARDIOVASCULAR RISK ASSOCIATED WITH RAPID INFUSION. Do not exceed IV phenytoin administration rates of 50 mg/min in adults or 1 to 3 mg/kg/min (or 50 mg/min, whichever is slower) in pediatric patients because rapid infusion can cause severe hypotension and cardiac arrhythmias. Monitor cardiac status during and after administration; reduce the rate or stop dosing if needed.","rationale":"FDA/openFDA confirms a boxed warning for IV phenytoin rapid infusion. DailyMed is not permissible for this field, and the current cell should use FDA/openFDA or accessdata FDA labeling metadata instead.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect"},"blocking_questions":["Decide whether placebo-controlled phenytoin/diphenylhydantoin trials for alcohol-withdrawal seizure recurrence are in scope for pubmed_phase_ii_iii_rct_links, or whether the list should be limited to epilepsy/neurosurgical/post-traumatic seizure prophylaxis."],"fact_checks":[{"current_value":"phenytoin","field":"generic_name","proposed_value":"phenytoin","rationale":"FDA labeling and PubMed records identify the drug as phenytoin/phenytoin sodium.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"diphenylhydantoin; phenytoin sodium","rationale":"Diphenylhydantoin is an established phenytoin alias in PubMed RCT records, and phenytoin sodium is the labeled salt form used for capsules/injection.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"diphenylhydantoin; diphenylhydantoin sodium; phenytoin sodium","rationale":"Missing diphenylhydantoin aliases can cause phenytoin RCTs to be missed or misclassified.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"missing","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/"]},{"current_value":"Dilantin; Epanutin; Phenytek; Phenytoin Sodium Flynn","field":"trade_names","proposed_value":"Dilantin; Epanutin; Phenytek; Phenytoin Sodium Flynn","rationale":"Dilantin/Phenytek are supported by US epilepsy/FDA-adjacent sources; Epanutin and Phenytoin Sodium Flynn are supported by eMC SmPC records.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.epilepsy.com/treatment/medicines/drug-interactions","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/7558/smpc","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/2257/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Yes","field":"available_in_us","proposed_value":"Yes","rationale":"FDA labels exist for Dilantin/phenytoin products in the US.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Capsule; chewable tablet; oral suspension; IV injection","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Capsule; chewable tablet; oral suspension; IV/IM injection","rationale":"US FDA labeling supports extended capsules, chewable tablets, oral suspension, and parenteral phenytoin sodium injection for intravenous or intramuscular use.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2015/084427Orig1s032lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2013/008762s047lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic; Status epilepticus; Neurosurgical seizure prophylaxis","field":"epilepsy_type","proposed_value":"Focal; Generalized tonic-clonic; Status epilepticus; Neurosurgical seizure prophylaxis","rationale":"Oral labeling supports tonic-clonic and psychomotor/temporal lobe seizures and neurosurgical seizure prevention/treatment; injection labeling supports generalized tonic-clonic status epilepticus.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Strong enzyme inducer: CYP3A4, CYP2C9/19, and UGT; nonlinear metabolism","field":"enzyme_inducing_or_inhibiting","proposed_value":"Strong CYP3A inducer and broad/potent hepatic enzyme inducer of CYP/UGT pathways; primarily metabolized by CYP2C9 and CYP2C19; saturable/nonlinear metabolism","rationale":"FDA supports phenytoin as a strong CYP3A inducer and potent hepatic enzyme inducer. PubMed review supports CYP/UGT induction. FDA/eMC labeling supports CYP2C9/CYP2C19 metabolism and saturable metabolism, but the current wording overstates CYP2C9/19 as strong induced enzymes.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-interactions-labeling/drug-development-and-drug-interactions-table-substrates-inhibitors-and-inducers","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9606477/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/7558/smpc"]},{"current_value":"7-42 h; concentration-dependent/nonlinear","field":"half_life_range","proposed_value":"7-42 h; concentration-dependent/nonlinear","rationale":"FDA oral labeling reports a 7 to 42 hour range and describes saturable hepatic metabolism with disproportionate serum-level increases after small dose increments.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Liver","field":"major_organ_for_metabolism","proposed_value":"Liver","rationale":"FDA/eMC labeling states phenytoin is primarily metabolized by hepatic CYP enzymes and that liver is the chief site of biotransformation.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/7558/smpc"]},{"current_value":"Precise mechanism is not established in labeling; thought to involve voltage-dependent blockade of membrane sodium channels, reducing sustained high-frequency neuronal discharges.","field":"mechanism_of_action","proposed_value":"Precise mechanism is not established in labeling; thought to involve voltage-dependent blockade of membrane sodium channels, reducing sustained high-frequency neuronal discharges.","rationale":"FDA labeling directly supports this mechanism wording.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"mechanism_source","proposed_value":"FDA accessdata labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","rationale":"DailyMed is not in the trusted source list supplied for this audit. FDA accessdata labeling supports the mechanism cell.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Moderate","field":"mechanism_confidence","proposed_value":"Moderate","rationale":"The label says the precise therapeutic mechanism is not established but describes a plausible sodium-channel mechanism, supporting moderate rather than high confidence.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"300 mg/day common adult maintenance starting regimen; individualized by levels","field":"minimum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Adult starting regimen: 100 mg three times daily (300 mg/day), individualized by serum levels","rationale":"FDA labeling gives 100 mg three times daily as the adult starting dose and recommends serum level monitoring for dosage adjustment.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Adults maintenance commonly 300-400 mg/day; individualized by levels","field":"typical_doses_per_day","proposed_value":"Adults maintenance commonly 300-400 mg/day; individualized by levels","rationale":"FDA labeling says most adults have satisfactory maintenance with one 100 mg capsule three to four times daily and dosage should be individualized.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Dose individualized by serum level; commonly up to 400-600 mg/day","field":"maximum_approved_daily_dose","proposed_value":"Dose individualized by serum level; adult dosing may be increased up to 600 mg/day if necessary","rationale":"FDA labeling permits an increase up to two 100 mg capsules three times daily if necessary.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"1938 legacy approval","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1953 (Initial U.S. Approval)","rationale":"FDA labeling lists Initial U.S. Approval as 1953; 1938 may refer to historical introduction/use but is not supported as the FDA clearance/approval year in the checked FDA labels.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"No typical QT prolongation; IV administration/toxicity can cause arrhythmias and conduction effects","field":"qt_interval_effect","proposed_value":"No typical QT-prolongation warning identified in checked FDA labeling; IV administration/toxicity can cause serious arrhythmias and conduction effects including bradycardia, heart block, ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation, cardiac arrest, and severe hypotension","rationale":"FDA labeling supports serious cardiac arrhythmia/conduction risk with IV or toxic phenytoin. The row's separate filter_qt_effect should not include QT prolongation unless a specific source is added.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Neurologic: nystagmus 20-40%, neurologic: ataxia 10-30%, CNS: dizziness 10-20%, gingival: gingival hyperplasia 20%, dermatologic: rash 5-10%, dermatologic: SJS/TEN <1%","field":"adverse_symptoms_percentages","proposed_value":"Neurologic/CNS: nystagmus, ataxia, slurred speech/decreased coordination, somnolence/mental confusion, dizziness/vertigo (common or dose-related; exact percentages not consistently quantified in checked FDA/NCBI sources); gingival: gingival hyperplasia/overgrowth reported (13% clinically significant in one community study; estimates up to 50% in reviews); dermatologic: rash reported (morbilliform rash 2-5% in IARC review; LiverTox notes rash can occur in 10%); severe dermatologic: SJS/TEN rare serious reactions","rationale":"Checked sources support the adverse event types but not the current exact nystagmus/ataxia/dizziness percentages. NCBI sources give different gingival/rash estimates and describe SJS/TEN as rare serious reactions.","requires_user_approval":true,"status":"insufficient_evidence","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548889/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK423938/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482444/"]},{"current_value":"NCBI LiverTox anticonvulsants table; Epilepsy Society ASM list; Epilepsy Foundation Australia ASM list; FDA/DailyMed labeling; Sills and Rogawski 2020 ASM mechanism review","field":"evidence_sources","proposed_value":"FDA accessdata labeling; FDA/openFDA drug label API for boxed warning; NCBI Bookshelf LiverTox/StatPearls/IARC phenytoin monographs; PubMed RCT records; eMC SmPC records for UK trade names; FDA drug-interaction table","rationale":"Several current named sources are not in the trusted-source domain list or do not support the specific FDA boxed-warning field. Replace with sources actually used for checked cells.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"incorrect","supporting_sources":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb%22&limit=5","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK548889/","https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK423938/","https://www.medicines.org.uk/emc/product/7558/smpc"]}],"generic_name":"phenytoin","missing_rcts":[{"pmid":"4610196","proposed_link_text":"Sampliner1974|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","title":"Diphenylhydantoin control of alcohol withdrawal seizures. Results of a controlled study","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","why_relevant":"Diphenylhydantoin is a phenytoin alias. PubMed classifies the report as Clinical Trial and Randomized Controlled Trial with placebo and seizure-prevention MeSH terms; include if alcohol-withdrawal seizure trials are in row scope."},{"pmid":"2686433","proposed_link_text":"Alldredge1989|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/","title":"Placebo-controlled trial of intravenous diphenylhydantoin for short-term treatment of alcohol withdrawal seizures","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/","why_relevant":"Prospective randomized double-blind placebo-controlled IV diphenylhydantoin/phenytoin trial for recurrent alcohol-withdrawal seizures; include if provoked seizure trials are in scope."},{"pmid":"2024792","proposed_link_text":"Chance1991|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2024792/","title":"Emergency department treatment of alcohol withdrawal seizures with phenytoin","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2024792/","why_relevant":"Prospective randomized placebo-controlled double-blind phenytoin trial for recurrence of alcohol-withdrawal seizures; include if provoked seizure trials are in scope."}],"outcome_checks":[{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_50_responder_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo RR50 differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"The retained phenytoin placebo RCTs are seizure-prevention/prophylaxis trials and do not report chronic-epilepsy RR50 responder outcomes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"]},{"current_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included phase II/III placebo-controlled RCT records","field":"diff_median_pct_change_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"NR/not extractable as a drug-minus-placebo MPC differential at the maximum effective dose/regimen from included placebo-controlled RCT records","rationale":"The retained phenytoin placebo RCTs do not provide extractable median percent seizure-frequency change outcomes.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/"]},{"current_value":"8.8-10.6 % (drug minus placebo seizure-freedom differential at maximum effective dose/regimen: Temkin1990 phenytoin high-therapeutic serum range after serious head trauma 10.6%; North1980 post-craniotomy phenytoin prophylaxis 8.8%)","field":"diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"8.8-10.6 % (drug minus placebo seizure/epilepsy-free differential derived from reported seizure/epilepsy rates: Temkin1990 96.4% vs 85.8% seizure-free through day 7, differential 10.6%; North1980 92.1% vs 83.3% epilepsy-free after craniotomy, differential 8.8%)","rationale":"Temkin reports seizures in 3.6% with phenytoin vs 14.2% with placebo; North reports postoperative epilepsy in 7.9% with phenytoin vs 16.7% with placebo. The CSV differentials are arithmetically correct when converted to seizure/epilepsy-free rates.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/"]},{"current_value":"Temkin1990|10.6|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/|404; North1980|8.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/|203","field":"plot_diff_seizure_freedom_maximum_effective_dose","proposed_value":"Temkin1990|10.6|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/|404; North1980|8.8|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/|203","rationale":"Sample sizes and seizure-free differentials match the PubMed abstracts/source evidence: Temkin n=404; North n=203.","requires_user_approval":false,"status":"verified","supporting_sources":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/"]}],"overall_status":"needs_update","proposed_row_updates":[{"current_value":"FDA/DailyMed SPL; status=boxed_warning_found; setid=a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb; ... url=https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb","field":"fda_black_box_warning_source","proposed_value":"FDA/openFDA drug label API; status=boxed_warning_found; spl_set_id=a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb; effective_time=20241226; title=PHENYTOIN SODIUM / PHENYTOIN SODIUM; api_url=https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb%22&limit=5","reason":"DailyMed is not permissible for FDA boxed-warning verification; use FDA/openFDA metadata.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"Current long boxed-warning text with duplicated heading and DailyMed-derived typographic inconsistencies","field":"fda_black_box_warning","proposed_value":"WARNING: CARDIOVASCULAR RISK ASSOCIATED WITH RAPID INFUSION. Do not exceed IV phenytoin administration rates of 50 mg/min in adults or 1 to 3 mg/kg/min (or 50 mg/min, whichever is slower) in pediatric patients because rapid infusion can cause severe hypotension and cardiac arrhythmias. Monitor cardiac status during and after administration; reduce the rate or stop dosing if needed.","reason":"Normalizes the boxed warning to FDA-supported content and removes duplicated/erroneous wording.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf","https://api.fda.gov/drug/label.json?search=openfda.spl_set_id%3A%22a52cf1bd-dc1f-47be-aba8-29066d9a50fb%22&limit=5"]},{"current_value":"1938 legacy approval","field":"year_fda_cleared","proposed_value":"1953 (Initial U.S. Approval)","reason":"Checked FDA labels list 1953 as Initial U.S. Approval.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"alternate_generic_names","proposed_value":"diphenylhydantoin; phenytoin sodium","reason":"Adds important alias/salt names to prevent duplicate-row or PubMed-search misses.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"","field":"pubmed_search_aliases","proposed_value":"diphenylhydantoin; diphenylhydantoin sodium; phenytoin sodium","reason":"Diphenylhydantoin appears in placebo-controlled phenytoin RCT titles/records.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/"]},{"current_value":"Capsule; chewable tablet; oral suspension; IV injection","field":"formulations_available","proposed_value":"Capsule; chewable tablet; oral suspension; IV/IM injection","reason":"Parenteral phenytoin sodium injection labeling includes IV or IM use.","requires_user_approval":false,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Other / unclear","field":"filter_mechanism","proposed_value":"Sodium channel blocker","reason":"FDA labeling supports voltage-dependent sodium channel blockade as the thought mechanism, while noting the precise mechanism is not established.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution; QT prolongation","field":"filter_qt_effect","proposed_value":"Conduction/arrhythmia caution","reason":"Checked FDA labeling supports arrhythmia/conduction risk but not a QT-prolongation classification.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2017/010151s047s048lbl.pdf","https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2022/084349s088lbl.pdf"]},{"current_value":"Young2004|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15039684/; DeSantis2002|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11903465/; Dikmen1991|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1995974/; Temkin1990|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/; Bacon1981|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/; North1980|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/","field":"pubmed_phase_ii_iii_rct_links","proposed_value":"Young2004|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15039684/; Dikmen1991|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1995974/; Temkin1990|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/; Bacon1981|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6116084/; North1980|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101843/; Sampliner1974|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/; Alldredge1989|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/; Chance1991|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2024792/","reason":"DeSantis2002 is randomized open-label/no-treatment control rather than placebo-controlled. The three added records are phenytoin/diphenylhydantoin placebo-controlled RCTs if alcohol-withdrawal seizure trials are in scope.","requires_user_approval":true,"source_urls":["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11903465/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4610196/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2686433/","https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2024792/"]}],"rct_link_checks":[{"label":"Young2004","pmid":"15039684","proposed_action":"Retain; no extractable maximum-effect RR50/MPC/SF value used in CSV summary.","rationale":"Title and PubMed metadata identify a randomized, double-blinded, placebo-controlled phenytoin trial for early posttraumatic seizure prevention in children.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15039684/"},{"label":"DeSantis2002","pmid":"11903465","proposed_action":"Remove from placebo-controlled RCT list or move to a non-placebo randomized-control field; user approval required because this deletes an existing link.","rationale":"PubMed abstract states the design was prospective open-label controlled; the control group did not receive phenytoin, so this is not a placebo-controlled trial despite randomization.","status":"not_placebo_rct","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11903465/"},{"label":"Dikmen1991","pmid":"1995974","proposed_action":"Retain as a placebo-controlled randomized phenytoin safety/neurobehavioral report; do not use for RR50/MPC/SF efficacy extraction.","rationale":"Report states subjects were randomized to phenytoin or placebo for posttraumatic seizure prophylaxis, but outcomes are neurobehavioral rather than seizure efficacy.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1995974/"},{"label":"Temkin1990","pmid":"2115976","proposed_action":"Retain; use for derived 10.6 percentage-point seizure-free differential.","rationale":"Randomized double-blind phenytoin vs placebo trial in 404 serious head trauma patients; PubMed reports 3.6% vs 14.2% early seizure rates.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2115976/"},{"label":"Bacon1981","pmid":"6116084","proposed_action":"Retain; phenytoin ineffective and extractable RR50/MPC/SF not available from checked abstract.","rationale":"Placebo-controlled study of phenobarbitone and phenytoin for febrile-convulsion prophylaxis with phenytoin and placebo arms.","status":"verified","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.n